WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 234: Greece: The Golden Age of Pericles: The Parthenon, Socrates, and Athenian Democracy

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.080
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:20.160
[Rebecca] Today, our journey takes us back two and a half millennia to the rocky promontories of Attica and the sunlit marble temples of classical Athens to explore the miraculous birthplace of Western philosophy, drama, and democracy.

3
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.040
[Rebecca] The Golden Age of Pericles.

4
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:40.960
[George] In the 5th century before Christ, on a rocky, arid peninsula jutting into the Aegean Sea, a small city state of barely a quarter million citizens achieved an intellectual and artistic explosion whose cultural radiation illuminates human civilization to this day.

5
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:49.920
[George] Following the heroic defeat of the invading Persian Persian armies at Marathon and Salamis, Athens underwent a radical political revolution.

6
00:00:49.920 --> 00:01:02.400
[George] Led by the democratic reforms of Clesthenes and Ephialtes, Athens abolished aristocratic oligarchy and established the world's first direct democracy, democratia, meaning the power of the people.

7
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:17.920
[George] Every free adult male Athenian citizen, regardless of wealth or lineage, possessed the right and duty to climb the rocky crest of the Penyx Hill, raise his hand, and vote directly on state laws, military declarations, and public budgets.

8
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:32.720
[George] To ensure that government was not monopolized by the rich, public magistrates, counselors, and jurors were chosen not by election, but by the democratic randomness of the Cleroterian, a stone lottery machine using white and black marbles.

9
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:39.840
[George] In the Athenian assembly, freedom of speech, Isgoria was the sacred birthright of every citizen.

10
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:52.400
[George] The democratic assembly on the Pennyx was summoned by the sound of heralds walking through the agora holding ropes dipped in wet red dye, herding citizens toward the rock by marking the cloaks of latecomers with red chalk.

11
00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:05.480
[George] The democratic citizens of Athens gathered ten times a year on the Penyx, voting by secret ballot using bronze tokens stamped with the Gorgon's head to ostracize and banish corrupt politicians for a period of ten years.

12
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:20.040
[George] For over thirty glorious years, from 461 to 429 BC, Athenian democracy was guided by the moral authority, aristocratic elegance, and towering intellect of one man, Pericles.

13
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:31.480
[George] Born into the noble Alcmeonid family, Pericles was a disciple of philosophers like Anaxagoras, possessing a dignified, calm demeanor that earned him the nickname, the Olympian.

14
00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:41.080
[George] Pericles believed passionately that democracy could only succeed if ordinary citizens were educated, cultured, and financially capable of public participation.

15
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:47.560
[George] He instituted daily state stipends for citizens serving on juries and attending religious theater festivals.

16
00:02:47.560 --> 00:03:00.920
[George] In his immortal funeral oration, delivered in the winter of 431 BC to honor the first Athenian soldiers fallen in the Peloponnesian War, Pericles articulated the timeless creed of an open society.

17
00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.920
[George] Our constitution does not copy the laws of neighboring states.

18
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.240
[George] We are rather a pattern to others.

19
00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:15.640
[George] We regard wealth as something to be properly used, not as something to boast about, and as for poverty, there is no shame in acknowledging it.

20
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.480
[George] The real shame is in not taking practical steps to escape it.

21
00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.720
[George] Athens is the school of Hellas.

22
00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:35.960
[George] Pericles' companion Aspasia of Miletus was celebrated for her brilliant philosophical mind and rhetorical eloquence, hosting lively intellectual salons attended by Socrates, Anaxagoras, and prominent Athenian statesmen.

23
00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:48.400
[George] Espasia's influence over Pericles was so profound that political satirists like Aristophanes claimed she was the true author of Pericles's speeches and the intellectual architect of the Golden Age of Athens.

24
00:03:48.400 --> 00:04:00.880
[George] Pericles envisioned Athens as a physical monument to human excellence, convincing the assembly to divert the treasury of the Delian League to rebuild the Acropolis, which had been burned to ashes by Persian invaders.

25
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:08.560
[George] He appointed the master sculptor Phidias as director of works, alongside the brilliant architects Actinus and Callicrates.

26
00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:27.200
[George] Over nine extraordinary years, thousands of stonemasons, carvers, and laborers quarried twenty thousand tons of luminous white pentelic marble from Mount Pentelicon, hauling colossal stone drums up the rocky Acropolis to erect the Parthenon, the temple dedicated to Athena Parthenos.

27
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:42.160
[George] The building was an architectural miracle of mathematical subtlety, recognizing that perfectly straight lines appear curved and sagging to the human eye, the architects designed the temple with optical refinements, the base was subtly curved upward.

28
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:53.840
[George] The columns swelled gently in the middle with entesis, and the corner columns leaned inward by several inches, giving the massive marble temple an illusion of weightless, organic vitality.

29
00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:02.320
[George] Inside stood Phidias's forty-foot-tall Chryselephantine statue of Athena, shimmering in solid sheets of pure gold and ivory.

30
00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:16.000
[George] The pentelic marble blocks for the Parthenon were transported along special inclined ramps using wooden rollers and heavy ropes, carved on site by hundreds of sculptors who received equal daily wages regardless of social status.

31
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] The Parthenon frieze depicted the majestic Panathenaic procession, showing Athenian citizens, cavalry riders, sacrificial heifers, and maidens ascending the Acropolis to present the goddess Athena with a sacred woven peplos.

32
00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:42.280
[George] On the southern slope of the Acropolis, in the open-air stone amphitheater of the theater of Dionysus, Athenian democracy invented drama as a sacred civic ritual of moral introspection.

33
00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:56.280
[George] Each spring during the city Dionysia festival, fifteen thousand citizens gathered to sit on limestone benches from dawn to dusk, witnessing the poetic masterpieces of the tragic poets Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides.

34
00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:11.240
[George] In plays like Oedipus Rex, Antigone and the Orstea, playwrights grappled with the agonies of human destiny, the conflict between divine law and tyrannical state power, the horrors of war, and the tragic consequences of hubris.

35
00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:24.360
[George] In comedy, Aristophanes subjected the most powerful politicians, generals and philosophers of Athens to savage, bawdy satire, mocking warmongers and corrupt demagogues before the laughing assembly.

36
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:35.880
[George] Athenian theatre was not mere escapist entertainment, but a profound democratic crucible where citizens looked into the mirror of human fallibility and refined their collective moral conscience.

37
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:47.400
[George] Tragic actors performed wearing oversized painted linen masks with wide funnel-shaped mouths that acted as primitive megaphones, allowing their voices to carry to the highest tiers of the theater.

38
00:06:47.400 --> 00:07:00.040
[George] Sophocles was not merely a brilliant poet but a revered general and statesman, serving alongside Pericles during the Samian War while composing masterpieces that explored the tragic limits of human pride.

39
00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:16.560
[George] Down in the crowded, bustling market square of the agora, amidst the stalls of olive oil merchants and shoemakers, an unkempt, barefoot stonecutter with a snub nose and bulging eyes spent his days challenging the intellectual foundations of the city, Socrates.

40
00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:26.640
[George] Rejecting the pre-Socratic obsession with cosmology, Socrates turned philosophy inward, famously declaring that the unexamined life is not worth living.

41
00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:44.960
[George] Using his revolutionary elenchus, the Socratic method of relentless, probing questioning, he cross-examined politicians, poets, and sophists who claimed to possess wisdom, exposing their superficial contradictions and revealing that true wisdom begins with the humble admission of one's own ignorance.

42
00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.800
[George] I know that I know nothing.

43
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.960
[George] Socrates charged no fees and wrote down not a single word.

44
00:07:50.960 --> 00:08:05.520
[George] Viewing himself as an intellectual gadfly sent by the gods to sting the great, sluggish Athenian horse into wakefulness, demanding that men seek virtue, truth, and the health of their immortal souls above wealth and political power.

45
00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:16.960
[George] Socrates spent his youth studying the natural philosophy of Archelaus before concluding that the study of trees and stones could never teach a man how to live a good, virtuous, and just life.

46
00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:29.200
[George] Socrates compared himself to a midwife like his mother Phenerete, explaining that while he possessed no wisdom of his own, his philosophical questioning helped others give birth to the latent truth within their souls.

47
00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:37.440
[George] The golden zenith of classical Athens was tragically cut short by the catastrophic Peloponnesian war against its martial rival, Sparta.

48
00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:44.560
[George] In 431 BC, Spartan hoplites marched into Attica, burning olive groves and crops.

49
00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:56.320
[George] Pericles ordered the entire rural population of Attica to retreat inside the long walls connecting Athens to its port of Piraeus, relying on the Athenian navy to supply the city from the sea.

50
00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:12.360
[George] But in the overcrowded, sweltering conditions of the walled city, a devastating epidemic, the plague of Athens, likely Typhus or Ebola, struck in 430 BC, killing one third of the population, including Pericles and his sons.

51
00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:16.120
[George] The social fabric of Athens disintegrated into lawlessness.

52
00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:30.920
[George] Demagogues like Cleon and the reckless, handsome Alcibiades led the city into imperial over expansion and moral degradation, culminating in the genocidal destruction of the island of Milos and the disastrous Sicilian expedition.

53
00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:41.960
[George] In 404 BC, after twenty-seven years of ruinous war, the starving Athenians surrendered to Sparta, and the long walls were demolished to the sound of Spartan flutes.

54
00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:54.760
[George] The Peloponnesian War forced thousands of rural refugees to live in wooden huts and ceramic storage jars cramped within the stifling space between the long walls, creating a deadly breeding ground for the epidemic.

55
00:09:54.760 --> 00:10:09.640
[George] The devastating epidemic of 430 BC undermined Athenian moral restraint, as citizens expecting to die within days abandoned religious piety, squandered their fortunes, and disregarded civil law in hedonistic despair.

56
00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:16.680
[George] In the bitter, paranoid aftermath of defeat, a traumatized Athenian democracy turned upon its own conscience.

57
00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:25.560
[George] In 399 BC, Socrates was brought to trial on fabricated charges of corrupting the youth and introducing strange gods.

58
00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:36.280
[George] Refusing to flatter the jury or plead for mercy, the seventy year old philosopher defended his divine mission of free inquiry before a jury of five hundred citizens.

59
00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:47.520
[George] Found guilty by a narrow margin, Socrates calmly drank a cup of poisonous hemlock in his prison cell, comforting his weeping disciples as the paralysis crept from his feet to his heart.

60
00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:50.720
[George] But the ideas of Socrates could not be executed.

61
00:10:50.720 --> 00:11:07.200
[George] His greatest disciple, Plato, founded the academy outside Athens, the first university of the Western world, where he composed immortal dialogues and taught Aristotle, who synthesized the encyclopedic foundations of science, logic, and ethics.

62
00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:16.880
[George] Classical Athens fell as an imperial power, but its intellectual, democratic, and artistic legacy conquered the minds of humanity for all eternity.

63
00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:30.720
[George] Plato immortalized Socrates's final hours in the Phaedo, describing how the philosopher bathed himself to save the women the trouble of washing his corpse, conversing calmly on the immortality of the soul until his final breath.

64
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.040
[George] Plato recorded Socrates's final words to his friend Credo.

65
00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:38.000
[George] Credo, we owe a cock to Asclepius.

66
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:44.720
[George] Do not forget to pay the debt, thanking the god of healing for curing him of the mortal illness of earthly life.

67
00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.040
[Rebecca] Next week, our Greek chronicle continues with episode 235.

68
00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:59.440
[Rebecca] Greece Part 2 Thermopylae and Salamis, King Leonidas, The Mysticles, and the Persian Defeat.

69
00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:02.080
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

